    "slug": "mudug-pastoralists-turn-to-fishing-as-drought-destroys-livestock",
    "summary": "In central Somalia's Mudug region, pastoralists who lost their livestock to drought are learning to fish along the coast, earning income in Fah, Hobyo, Elhur, Gal-hogag, Labad, and Harardere.",
    "content": "In the Mudug region of central Somalia, a number of men who previously herded livestock have shifted to fishing along the coast after successive droughts wiped out their animals. Many had never seen the sea before but are now learning to swim, dive, and fish in locations such as Fah, Hobyo, Elhur, Gal-hogag, Labad, and Harardere. The move comes as the pastoralist way of life becomes increasingly untenable due to repeated rain failures.\n\nOne such man is Osman Mahmoud Ali, 32, who lost all 60 of his goats to drought and moved to Hobyo. He now supports a family of 10 on an income of roughly $250 to $300 per month from fishing. Ali is among many who have relocated from rural areas to the coast in search of alternative livelihoods.\n\nThe drought cycle in central Somalia has been particularly severe in recent years, with back-to-back failed rainy seasons forcing many pastoralists to abandon their herds. Livestock is the backbone of the local economy, and the loss of animals has left families with few options. Fishing, traditionally a minor activity in the region, has emerged as a vital fallback.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the shift highlights the growing need for adaptable skills and diversified income sources back home. Many diaspora members send remittances to relatives in Mudug and other affected regions. The transition to fishing not only provides immediate income but also offers a potential pathway to long-term resilience if supported with training and equipment.\n\nThe diaspora can play a role by funding local fishing cooperatives, providing boats and gear, or supporting vocational training programs. As climate change continues to disrupt traditional livelihoods, such grassroots adaptations are likely to become more common, and diaspora investment could help sustain them.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/mudug-pastoralists-turn-to-fishing-after-losing-livestock-to-drought/",
